            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "MLFOUCHEY"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I'm from Detroit and I've thought this is how most people eat a Coney ! Of course the best 'chili' for them is the bulk 'dehydrated' chili you can get from Sherwood Foods. and lots of diced onion and good mustard like Plochmans . one can also put on some saurkraut ( if your from the Hamtramck area ! )"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Tim T Janson"
            },
            "reviewBody": "So in a time pinch using a canned chili sauce is ok. But you want to make sure you use one with REAL meat not those cheap ones that are just crushed beans that look like meat. Tony Packos is good. National Coney Island makes it in a tube which you need to add water for your desired thickness, or now they sell it in cans which is also good. But Real Chili is so easy to make. The trick is adding water to the ground beef when you start cooking it. The water breaks down the meat so you get a nice, smooth chili rather than a chunky chili sauce. Every one knows a Detroit dog has a nice smooth consistency. From there its just a matter of seasoning to your taste.",
            "datePublished": "2025-06-30T16:33:55.632Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Lucy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Yummy. Even better with Koegel hot dogs. Sure do miss Michigan",
            "datePublished": "2013-11-18T17:19:43.310Z"
          },
          {
